<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Beirut</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Beirut is a port city on the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> coast, now the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">medieval period</a> it was one of the coastal ports, along with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acre">Acre</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyre">Tyre</a>, that connected overland routes from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-gulf">Persian Gulf</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> to Mediterranean trade. Its commercial importance shaped the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubid Empire</a>'s relations with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusader</a> coastal enclaves.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/beirut.jpg" alt="Beirut" width="320" height="388" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: SCHW · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Beyrouth_vue_a%C3%A9rienne.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
